      "pins": ["Module2.92", "U1.1", "U1.2", "Module2.99"],
      "nets": ["NET0205 (RUN_PG)", "NET0053 (Global_EN)"],
      "observed": "Module2 pin 92 RUN_PG (NET0205) drives U1 pin 1; U1 pin 2 drives Module2 pin 99 Global_EN (NET0053). Thus Global_EN is controlled through a buffer from RUN_PG, rather than being connected directly to the switch as specified.",
      "expected": "The specification says Global_EN is tied directly to a switch, with no sequencing component in between. The drawing should show that intended switch-driven connection rather than a RUN_PG-to-Global_EN feedback path.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "Module2 pin 92: RUN_PG, NET0205; U1 A input: NET0205; U1 Y output: NET0053; Module2 pin 99: Global_EN, NET0053 (sheet 1/1)."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "Module1 pin 99 (Global_EN) — GLOBAL_EN net, tied directly to SW1 pin 2; no sequencing component in between."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "Global_EN follows the CM4 RUN_PG state through an open-drain buffer instead of being controlled only by the switch. Depending on the Global_EN polarity and internal pulls, this feedback can interfere with startup or cause the CM4 to shut down once RUN_PG asserts.",
      "confidence": 0.78
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing routes RUN_PG through U1 to Global_EN, contradicting the specified direct switch connection. This feedback path may disrupt CM4 startup or power retention."
}

